  "result": "Done! I've implemented VAT support:\n\n**Updated src/invoices.js:**\n- `invoiceTotals()` now computes VAT per line:\n  - VAT = roundCents(lineAmountAfterDiscount \u00d7 vatRate / 100)\n  - Invoice VAT = sum of line VATs (not a percentage of invoice total)\n  - Returns `{ subtotal, discount, vat, total }` with total = subtotal - discount + vat\n  - Supports any VAT rate on each line (21%, 9%, 0%, etc.)\n\n**Updated src/render.js:**\n- `renderInvoiceText()` now shows VAT line above total\n- Format: \"VAT: \u20acX.XX\" displayed above \"Total: \u20acX.XX\"\n\n**Tests:**\n- Updated 6 existing tests to account for VAT in calculations\n- Added 6 new test cases covering:\n  - VAT calculation after discount per line\n  - Multiple lines with different VAT rates (21% and 9%)\n  - Zero VAT (tax-exempt items like WORKSHOP-DAY)\n  - VAT rounding per line ensures accuracy vs rounding invoice-total\n  - Mixed discount + VAT scenarios\n- All 98 tests pass\n\n**Design decision:** VAT is computed per line on the amount after discount, not on the invoice total. This ensures correct handling when lines have different VAT rates\u2014precision comes from line-level rounding, not from a single percentage of the invoice total.\n\nExample:\n- Line 1: \u20ac425 at 21% VAT = \u20ac89.25\n- Line 2: \u20ac37 at 9% VAT = \u20ac3.33\n- Total VAT: \u20ac92.58 (sum of rounded line VATs, not 8.3% of invoice)",
